    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Not directly—my vocational diploma covers practical maths for trades or basic engineering, but I’ve used a free online solver (like Wolfram Alpha) occasionally to double-check calculations. The last time I tried an AI tutor (a flawed experiment), it failed to explain *why* an equation was invalid, just computed the result. My experience reinforced my distrust of tech when it lacks human intuition—especially since my low openness makes me skeptical of over-simplified answers.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second-order algebraic equation?": "Start with an equation like **ax² + bx + c = 0**. First, find the discriminant (**D = b² – 4ac**). If **D > 0**, factor as **(x – r₁)(x – r₂)**, where r₁ and r₂ are roots. For D ≤ 0, use the quadratic formula: **x = [-b ± √D] / (2a)**. My trick? Substitute real-world numbers (e.g., 3x² – 7x + 4) to connect maths to cooking measurements—I’d measure ingredients while solving!",
